<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Graves Simcoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1752">1752</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Graves Simcoe was a British army officer who commanded the Queen's Rangers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. As the first lieutenant governor of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1791 to 1796 he encouraged Loyalist settlement, laid out roads such as Yonge Street and moved the capital from Newark to York, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>. He also banned the import of slaves in 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe.jpg" alt="John Graves Simcoe" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Theodore Berthon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ColonelSimcoe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
